EXT. BORDER CHECKPOINT - DAWN

Fog lies on the valley like spilled milk. Two lanes of trucks idle in a row, exhaust rising straight up in the windless cold. A loudspeaker coughs somewhere, then gives up.

A faded sign reads: ALL VEHICLES STOP. Under it, a stray dog sleeps against a concrete barrier, ribs rising and falling.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Eleven years at this crossing, and I can tell you how a man lies by how he holds his steering wheel. Tight means fear. Loose means practice. This one held it like a baby bird.

A grey hatchback rolls to the booth. Behind the glass, INSPECTOR HALVORSEN, 50s, heavy, unhurried, a thermos steaming beside his keyboard, lifts one finger.

INT. HATCHBACK - CONTINUOUS

DELPHINE VASS, 30s, hair damp, scarf too bright for the hour, rolls down the window. In the back seat a child's booster seat sits empty.

HALVORSEN
Passport. Purpose of travel.

DELPHINE
Visiting my sister. Three days.

HALVORSEN
Where does your sister live?

DELPHINE
Kalmar. Near the harbour.

On the dashboard her phone lights up.

ON PHONE: where r u

She turns it face down. Halvorsen watches her do it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nothing wrong with that. Everybody's got somebody who wants to know where they are.

EXT. BORDER CHECKPOINT - CONTINUOUS

Two TEENAGERS in a van queue take photographs of the fog on their phones, laughing. A WAITER from the roadside cafe crosses the road carrying a tray of paper cups to the guards, steam curling off them.

Younger officer KESTREL OKONKWO, 20s, eager, jacket too clean, jogs over from the second booth.

KESTREL
Sir, the dog unit's flagged nothing on the left lane.

HALVORSEN
Good. Then we are all friends this morning.

He smiles at Delphine and slides her passport back through the slot.

HALVORSEN (CONT'D)
Safe travels, Ms. Vass.

DELPHINE
(exhaling)
Thank you.

The barrier arm lifts. The hatchback rolls forward ten feet, twenty. Her shoulders drop.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here is what I know about relief. It makes people careless with their hands.

Her phone buzzes again against the dash. She reaches for it without looking.

ON PHONE: did u get through?

She types with one thumb.

ON PHONE (CONT'D): yes. dont call me again

Halvorsen watches the hatchback in the mirror, thermos halfway to his lips.

HALVORSEN
Kestrel. The booster seat.

KESTREL
Sir?

HALVORSEN
Who rides to her sister's for three days with a child seat and no child?

KESTREL
(beat)
Maybe the kid's with the sister already?

HALVORSEN
Then why is the seat belt buckled across it?

Silence. The dog under the barrier lifts its head.

HALVORSEN (CONT'D)
Close the arm.

Kestrel hits a button. The barrier SLAMS down in front of the hatchback, fifteen feet from freedom. Brake lights flare red in the fog.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here is the thing nobody tells you. The calm is not the quiet part. The calm is the trap.

Halvorsen walks out of the booth, unhurried, and Kestrel falls into step beside him. They reach the driver's window together.

HALVORSEN
Ms. Vass. A moment.

KESTREL
Ma'am, please step out of the vehicle.

HALVORSEN
Please step out.

DELPHINE
(overlapping, shaking)
I have to be in Kalmar by noon. My sister is expecting me.

^DELPHINE
(overlapping, shaking)
I have to be in Kalmar by noon.

She does not move. Her hands are tight on the wheel. Not the baby-bird grip now. Something harder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There it is. Loose means practice. Tight means fear. Tight and then loose again, that means you have made up your mind.

Delphine puts the car in reverse.

Halvorsen steps back. Kestrel's hand drops to his belt.

HALVORSEN
(calm)
Don't.

The engine revs. Fog swallows the sound. Somewhere beyond the barrier, a child begins to cry, thin and far away, from a truck in the other lane.

Delphine's eyes go wide. She turns her head toward it.

DELPHINE
(whispering)
That's not -

The teenagers' phones all come up at once, filming.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Eleven years. I never once saw a border. Only the people standing very close to it.

HALVORSEN
(quietly)
Ms. Vass. Whose child is in that truck?

She doesn't answer. The engine cuts out. The only sound is the dog, finally, barking.
